An array is a collection of elements of the same type stored in a single variable. It allows you to access each element using an index. Arrays make it easy to store and manage multiple values together. On the basis of  Size: 1. Fixed Sized Arrays: The size of a fixed-size array cannot be changed after creation.Memory is allocated only for the size mentioned in square brackets [].Declaring a larger size than needed wastes memory.Declaring a smaller size than needed is not enough to store all elements.Fixed-size arrays are not preferred when the number of elements is unknown.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    // fixed-size array of 5 integers
    int arr[5];

    // storing values in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        arr[i] = i + 1;
    }

    // printing array elements
    cout << "Array elements are: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}


